ntrose: a long, low building of grey-painted cypress siding in a white steel frame, its roof of ferro-cement 'leaves' over glass run out on slender white columns as a porch all the way round, gardens let into the plan, the Treasure House (the storerooms) as a second floor over the middle; live oaks along Sul Ross and Branard, grey bungalows of the neighbourhood. Footprint and street positions from the ground file (about 136 x 36 m, long east-west); the rest from the Menil's and published descriptions. Guessed: the number and places of the gardens, the entrance position, the leaves' direction (drawn as east-west rows), heights (about 7 m; 13 m at the Treasure House). No photographs were available."}